<h1>TImage</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TImage" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TImage</tt> displays an image on a page. It renders an <tt>&lt;img&gt;</tt> element.
<tt>ImageUrl</tt> takes a relative or absolute URL to the image file and becomes the
<tt>src</tt> attribute. <tt>AlternateText</tt> becomes the <tt>alt</tt> attribute, which a
screen reader announces and a browser shows when the image fails to load. An image that
carries no meaning of its own takes an empty <tt>AlternateText</tt>, so that assistive
technology skips it.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TImage ImageUrl="logo.png" AlternateText="Company logo" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Data</tt> is another name for <tt>ImageUrl</tt>, which lets an image serve as the data
renderer of a templated control.
</p>

<h2>Two properties to avoid</h2>

<p class="block-content">
HTML5 dropped the attributes behind two of the control's properties, and the framework
marks both deprecated. They still render, so existing pages keep working.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Renders</th><th>Use instead</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ImageAlign</tt></td>
	<td><tt>align</tt></td>
	<td>The <tt>Style</tt> property, with the CSS <tt>float</tt> or <tt>vertical-align</tt> declaration.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>DescriptionUrl</tt></td>
	<td><tt>longdesc</tt></td>
	<td>An ARIA attribute such as <tt>aria-describedby</tt>, set through the <tt>Attributes</tt> collection.</td>
</tr>
</table>
